\section{The Rotated-Pentagon Profile}
\label{sec:rotated-regular-polygons}
\providecommand{\pentagonassetpath}{docs/pentagon-chapter/assets/}

We study how the capacity of a product of two regular pentagons changes
as one factor rotates relative to the other. Rotation preserves the
volume, so maximizing capacity also maximizes the systolic ratio. We
determine the full profile and show that its maximum occurs at the
relative angle of the Haim--Kislev--Ostrover counterexample.

Let $P_5\subset\mathbb R^2$ be a regular pentagon centred at the origin
with circumradius one. Write $R(\theta)$ for counterclockwise planar
rotation and set
\[
 K_\theta=P_5\times_L R(\theta)P_5
 \subset\mathbb R_q^2\oplus\mathbb R_p^2.
\]
Here the Lagrangian product is the Cartesian product in the indicated
coordinate planes, equipped with
\[
 \omega_0((q,p),(q',p'))=q\cdot p'-p\cdot q'.
\]
Simultaneous rotation of both planes is symplectic, so the initial
orientation of $P_5$ is immaterial. We use the normalization
\[
 \operatorname{sys}(K)=
 \frac{c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K)^2}{2\operatorname{vol}_4(K)}.
\]

\subsection{From sampled values to an exact profile}
\label{subsec:rotated-regular-polygons-empirical-curves}

Computing the systolic ratio as one pentagon rotates reveals the profile
in Figure~\ref{fig:rotated-pentagons-curve}: the ratio rises from the
aligned configuration to a maximum at $\pi/10$, then falls symmetrically.
These values suggested a formula proportional to $\sec^2\theta$ on
$[0,\pi/10]$. The computation also identified a candidate with exactly
this ratio. To prove the formula, we must show that no competing
candidate has smaller action at any angle in the interval.

Symmetry places the Haim--Kislev--Ostrover example at both endpoints of
$[-\pi/10,\pi/10]$. We will show that the same orders and weights remain feasible
as the pentagon rotates, and that each candidate's quadratic objective
depends on the angle through a first harmonic. Interpolating its endpoint
values then bounds its value throughout the interval.\footnote{The
endpoint-interpolation argument was supplied during an
AI-assisted review of the thesis in September 2026. The numerical profile
and the explicit attaining word were already present in the thesis.}

\begin{figure}[tbp]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.88\linewidth]{\pentagonassetpath rotation-profile.png}
 \caption{Computed systolic ratios for $K_\theta$ (blue markers) and the
 exact profile proved below (black curve), over one period.
 The horizontal line marks $\operatorname{sys}=1$.}
 \label{fig:rotated-pentagons-curve}
\end{figure}

Define the distance to the nearest multiple of $\pi/5$ by
\[
 d(\theta)=\min_{k\in\mathbb Z}
       \left|\theta-\frac{k\pi}{5}\right|,
 \qquad 0\le d(\theta)\le\frac{\pi}{10}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Rotated-pentagon profile]
\label{thm:rotated-pentagon-product-formula}
For every real $\theta$,
\begin{align}
 c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K_\theta)
 &=\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr)^2\sec d(\theta),
 \label{eq:pentagon-capacity-profile}\\
 \operatorname{sys}(K_\theta)
 &=\frac{5+2\sqrt5}{10}\sec^2 d(\theta).
 \label{eq:pentagon-systolic-profile}
\end{align}
The maximum systolic ratio in this family is
\[
 \max_{\theta\in\mathbb R}\operatorname{sys}(K_\theta)
 =\frac{3+\sqrt5}{5}>1,
\]
and it is attained exactly at
$\theta=\pi/10+k\pi/5$, $k\in\mathbb Z$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Symmetries and the known endpoint capacity}

The pentagon's rotational symmetry immediately gives
$K_{\theta+2\pi/5}=K_\theta$. If $S$ is reflection in a symmetry axis
of $P_5$, then $(q,p)\mapsto(Sq,Sp)$ is symplectic, since $S$ is
orthogonal. Using $SP_5=P_5$ and $SR(\theta)=R(-\theta)S$, it maps
$K_\theta$ to $K_{-\theta}$. In particular, capacity is even in $\theta$.

There is a further symmetry involving exchange of the factors. The
symplectic map $(q,p)\mapsto(p,-q)$ sends $K_\theta$ to
$R(\theta)P_5\times_L(-P_5)$. Rotating both factors by $-\theta$
then gives $K_{\pi/5-\theta}$: indeed,
$-P_5=R(\pi)P_5=R(\pi/5)P_5$. Combining this symmetry with evenness
shows that capacity has period $\pi/5$. The volume is constant, so the
same symmetries hold for the systolic ratio. It is enough to compute
capacity on $[-\pi/10,\pi/10]$.

Haim--Kislev and Ostrover use $\sum_i dp_i\wedge dq_i=-\omega_0$, and their
second pentagon is rotated by $-\pi/2$. The map
$(q,p)\mapsto(q,-p)$ identifies that symplectic space with ours and
sends their product to $K_{\pi/2}$. By the reflection just described,
this has the same capacity as $K_{-\pi/2}$. Proposition~1.4
of~\cite{HaimKislevOstrover2024} thus gives
\begin{equation}
 c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K_{\pi/10})
 =c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K_{-\pi/10})
 =2\cos(\pi/10)\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr),
 \label{eq:pentagon-known-endpoints}
\end{equation}
where $\pi/2$ and $\pi/10$ differ by $2\pi/5$.

\subsection{A common feasible set and harmonic interpolation}

To compare candidates at different angles, we fix the facet labels.
Put
\[
 h=\cos(\pi/5),\qquad
 n_i=\left(\cos\left(\frac\pi2+\frac{2\pi i}{5}\right),
           \sin\left(\frac\pi2+\frac{2\pi i}{5}\right)\right),
 \quad 0\le i<5.
\]
We choose $P_5=\{q:\langle n_i,q\rangle\le h,\ 0\le i<5\}$.
Its normalized facet normals, in $(q_1,q_2,p_1,p_2)$ coordinates, are
\[
 a_i(\theta)=h^{-1}(n_i,0),\qquad
 a_{i+5}(\theta)=h^{-1}(0,R(\theta)n_i),\qquad 0\le i<5.
\]
Thus $K_\theta$ is defined by the ten inequalities
$\langle a_i(\theta),x\rangle\le1$.

Recall the finite quadratic program in this normalization
\cite{HK2017}. A word $\sigma=(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m)$ is an
ordered list of distinct indices from $\{0,\ldots,9\}$. Its feasible
weights form the set
\begin{equation}
 M_\sigma(\theta)=\left\{\beta\in\mathbb R_{\ge0}^m:
 \sum_{k=1}^m\beta_k=1,\quad
 \sum_{k=1}^m\beta_k a_{\sigma_k}(\theta)=0\right\}.
 \label{eq:pentagon-feasibility}
\end{equation}
For a word and weights, define its angle-dependent quadratic value by
\begin{equation}
 Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\theta)=
 \sum_{1\le j<k\le m}\beta_j\beta_k
 \omega_0\bigl(a_{\sigma_j}(\theta),a_{\sigma_k}(\theta)\bigr).
 \label{eq:pentagon-q-definition}
\end{equation}
The ordering in this formula is the traversal convention used in the
earlier quadratic-program chapter. Write
\begin{equation}
 Q_{\max}(\theta)=
 \max_{\substack{\sigma,\ \beta\in M_\sigma(\theta)}}
 Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\theta),\qquad
 c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K_\theta)=\frac{1}{2Q_{\max}(\theta)}.
 \label{eq:pentagon-hk}
\end{equation}
The maximum ranges over words with nonempty feasible sets. For each
such word, the feasible set is a closed subset of the standard simplex
in $\mathbb R^m$, hence compact, and the objective is continuous.
There are only finitely many words of distinct indices. The maximum
therefore exists, and the capacity formula asserts that it is positive.
Allowing zero weights includes candidates using fewer facets.

Closure in~\eqref{eq:pentagon-feasibility} separates into the two equations
\[
 \sum_{\sigma_k<5}\beta_k n_{\sigma_k}=0,
 \qquad
 R(\theta)\sum_{\sigma_k\ge5}\beta_k n_{\sigma_k-5}=0.
\]
Since $R(\theta)$ is invertible, it can be removed from the second
equation. The constraints are thus independent of $\theta$, and we
write their common feasible set as $M_\sigma$. This permits us to
compare exactly the same word and weights at different angles.

Fix any $\sigma$ and $\beta\in M_\sigma$. Same-factor symplectic
pairings vanish. Every mixed pairing is, up to sign,
$h^{-2}n_i\cdot R(\theta)n_j$. Consequently there are constants
$A_{\sigma,\beta},B_{\sigma,\beta}\in\mathbb R$ such that
\begin{equation}
 Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\theta)=
 A_{\sigma,\beta}\cos\theta+B_{\sigma,\beta}\sin\theta.
 \label{eq:pentagon-first-harmonic}
\end{equation}
For $-\pi/10\le\theta\le\pi/10$, the first harmonic satisfies
\begin{align}
 Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\theta)
 &=\frac{\sin(\pi/10-\theta)}{\sin(\pi/5)}
       Q_{\sigma,\beta}(-\pi/10)\notag\\
 &\quad+\frac{\sin(\pi/10+\theta)}{\sin(\pi/5)}
       Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\pi/10).
 \label{eq:pentagon-interpolation}
\end{align}
One obtains the identity by substituting~\eqref{eq:pentagon-first-harmonic}
and using the sine addition formulas. Both coefficients are nonnegative
on this interval, and their sum is $\cos\theta/\cos(\pi/10)$.
By~\eqref{eq:pentagon-known-endpoints} and~\eqref{eq:pentagon-hk},
each endpoint value on the right is at most
\[
 \frac{1}{4\cos(\pi/10)\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr)}.
\]
We may substitute these two bounds in~\eqref{eq:pentagon-interpolation}
because its coefficients are nonnegative. Using
$2\cos^2(\pi/10)=1+\cos(\pi/5)$ gives
\begin{equation}
 Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\theta)
 \le\frac{\cos\theta}{2\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr)^2}.
 \label{eq:pentagon-all-candidate-bound}
\end{equation}
Taking the maximum gives the same upper bound for $Q_{\max}(\theta)$.
Since capacity is its reciprocal as in~\eqref{eq:pentagon-hk}, this
is a lower bound on capacity.

To attain the bound, consider the word and weights
\[
 \sigma=(3,8,1,0,5,6),\qquad
 \beta=\left(\frac{\sqrt5}{10},\frac{\sqrt5}{10},
 \frac{5-\sqrt5}{20},\frac{5-\sqrt5}{20},
 \frac{5-\sqrt5}{20},\frac{5-\sqrt5}{20}\right).
\]
All weights are positive and their sum is one. The identities
\[
 n_0+n_1=-2h n_3,\qquad
 2h\,\frac{5-\sqrt5}{20}=\frac{\sqrt5}{10}
\]
give closure in each factor, so $\beta\in M_\sigma$.
We can compute its objective by grouping consecutive entries from
the same factor. Their mutual pairings vanish, and bilinearity allows
their weighted normals to be added in all the other pairings. With
\[
 u=\frac{\sqrt5}{10h}n_3,\qquad v=R(\theta)u,
\]
the four groups, in order, are
\[
 (u,0),\quad(0,v),\quad(-u,0),\quad(0,-v).
\]
The sum of their ordered pairings is $2u\cdot v$. Therefore
\begin{equation}
 Q_{\sigma,\beta}(\theta)
 =2\left(\frac{\sqrt5}{10h}\right)^2\cos\theta
 =\frac{\cos\theta}{2\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr)^2},
 \label{eq:pentagon-attaining-word}
\end{equation}
where the last equality follows from $h=(1+\sqrt5)/4$.
Combining~\eqref{eq:pentagon-all-candidate-bound}
and~\eqref{eq:pentagon-attaining-word} proves
\[
 c_{\mathrm{EHZ}}(K_\theta)
 =\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr)^2\sec\theta,
 \qquad |\theta|\le\pi/10.
\]
Evenness and $\pi/5$-periodicity now give
\eqref{eq:pentagon-capacity-profile} for every $\theta\in\mathbb R$.

It remains to normalize by volume. The pentagon consists of five
triangles with two unit sides and included angle $2\pi/5$, so
\[
 \operatorname{area}(P_5)=\frac52\sin(2\pi/5),\qquad
 \operatorname{vol}_4(K_\theta)=
       \left(\frac52\sin(2\pi/5)\right)^2.
\]
Consequently
\[
 \operatorname{sys}(K_\theta)=
 \frac{\bigl(1+\cos(\pi/5)\bigr)^4}
 {2\bigl(\frac52\sin(2\pi/5)\bigr)^2}\sec^2 d(\theta)
 =\frac{5+2\sqrt5}{10}\sec^2 d(\theta).
\]
The function $\sec^2 t$ is strictly increasing for
$0\le t\le\pi/10$. Its endpoint value yields
$(3+\sqrt5)/5$, proving both the maximum and its equality cases in
Theorem~\ref{thm:rotated-pentagon-product-formula}.\hfill$\square$
